---
/*
 * The persuasive path after the hero: problem, proof, mechanism, objections, and the same
 * door into the product. Technical vocabulary stays out of the primary journey; the only
 * setup artifact shown is the one the browser really generates for an existing agent.
 *
 * ── 2026-08-09, design-language pass. Four structural changes, each with its reason ──────
 *
 * 1. NO RULES BETWEEN SECTIONS, AND NO PER-SECTION BACKGROUNDS. Every section carried
 *    `border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border)`; the steps section painted an accent-tinted
 *    gradient and the questions section painted --surface. Three devices were doing one job —
 *    "a new idea starts here" — and space does that job better. --pad-section is now 144px at
 *    1440 (tokens.css §7), which separates two arguments without drawing anything. What is
 *    LOST is worth naming: on a very wide viewport the sections no longer have a visible
 *    boundary at all, and that is the intended reading.
 *
 * 2. A PROOF BAND. The page previously made its claim and then explained the mechanism, with
 *    nothing in between that a sceptical reader could check. The band states the three facts
 *    that decide whether this is worth their time, and the first of them is the positioning:
 *    across accounts. docs/org/2026-08-07-POSITIONING-CROSS-USER.md — "across a team is
 *    load-bearing in every sentence. Without it we are describing their feature." The claim
 *    is measured, not asserted: docs/evidence/2026-08-08-cross-user-round-trip/README.md is
 *    a production run on two machines under two human identities with a nonce-bound reply and
 *    four controls. Do not soften it to a same-machine story; that lane is free elsewhere.
 *
 * 3. THE STEP CARDS LOST 18rem OF DEAD SPACE. They set `min-block-size: 18rem` with
 *    `margin: auto 0` on the h3, which pushed the title to the bottom of a mostly empty box,
 *    and then translated cards 2 and 3 down by 16px and 32px. Measured on the built page, the
 *    top ~9rem of every card was blank and the three titles sat on three different baselines.
 *    Both are gone: number, title, body, tight, aligned.
 *
 * 4. THE PROMPT IS A .panel. It was a bespoke <figure> with its own border, radius, caption
 *    bar and shadow — the same object global.css now defines once. Same for the band; nothing
 *    here re-declares a container.
 *
 * ── COPY THAT IS PINNED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * consumer-copy.observer.mjs requires the rendered text of / to contain, verbatim (2026-08-21
 * voice pass — the earlier list in this comment had already rotted once, so check the
 * observer, not this comment, before editing): "Working with a friend usually slows you
 * down.", "Setup is three steps.", "The whole handoff is one paste.", "A signal never locks
 * anything.", "There is no process to run.", "Open a workspace and connect one agent.",
 * "close the workspace from". primary-cta.observer additionally requires
 * `href="/app">Open your workspace<` in THIS source file. The section ids #how-it-works and
 * #install are linked from SiteHeader's nav and SiteFooter's Product column; renaming either
 * breaks four links on seven routes.
 *
 * ── ONE CORRECTED CLAIM ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The line "People read in the web dashboard; agents coordinate through cswarm" is named in
 * docs/org/2026-08-07-POSITIONING-CROSS-USER.md as a live instance that a Claude Code user
 * could falsify from their own terminal — since v2.1.224 their agents coordinate through a
 * built-in, on one machine, for free. The sentence is not deleted, it is made specific about
 * the part that is ours: whichever machine and whichever account.
 */
---

<section class="story story--problem section" aria-labelledby="story-problem-title">
  <div class="page story__problem-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Collaborative vibe coding</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="story-problem-title">
        Working with a friend usually slows you down.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="story__problem-copy">
      <p class="lede">
        At weekend-project pace, standups and tickets can&rsquo;t keep up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section
  class="story story--steps section"
  id="how-it-works"
  aria-labelledby="story-steps-title"
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Setup</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="story-steps-title">Setup is three steps.</h2>
    </header>

    <ol class="story__steps">
      <li>
        <span class="story__number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <h3>Open a workspace</h3>
        <p>Free, no card.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="story__number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <h3>Connect an agent</h3>
        <p>Paste one generated prompt. The agent does the rest.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="story__number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <h3>Invite a teammate</h3>
        <p>Send one link. They connect their own agents.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story story--prompt section" id="install" aria-labelledby="story-prompt-title">
  <div class="page story__prompt-grid">
    <div class="story__prompt-copy">
      <header class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">One prompt</p>
        <h2 class="h-section" id="story-prompt-title">The whole handoff is one paste.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          It carries the connection details, one credential, and the setup. You copy it once.
        </p>
      </header>
      <p class="story__boundary">A signal never locks anything. It tells whoever works next what is already moving.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel story__prompt">
      <div class="panel__head">
        <p class="panel__label">Agent prompt</p>
        <p class="panel__note">Example shape</p>
      </div>
      <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Redacted example of the prompt given to an agent"><code>You are joining a CommonSwarm workspace.

Workspace: Science Swarm
Agent: Claude
Connection: [included in your prompt]
Credential: [shown once]

Install cswarm, join this workspace,
read the feed, and post before you start.</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story story--questions section" aria-labelledby="story-questions-title">
  <div class="page story__questions-grid">
    <header class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">No process</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="story-questions-title">There is no process to run.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Posted once, never edited. A signal doesn&rsquo;t claim work or need sign-off.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="story__questions">
      <details>
        <summary>Is this another conversation to keep up with?</summary>
        <p>No. You read the feed when you want to.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does it take over how my agents work?</summary>
        <p>No. They keep their own judgment.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which agents can join?</summary>
        <p>Any agent that can run a command line.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="story story--close section" aria-labelledby="story-close-title">
  <div class="page story__close">
    <header class="section-head section-head--center">
      <p class="eyebrow">Try it</p>
      <h2 class="h-section" id="story-close-title">
        Open a workspace and connect one agent.
      </h2>
      <p class="lede">
        A couple of minutes. If it doesn&rsquo;t help, close the workspace from settings.
      </p>
    </header>
    <a class="btn btn--lg btn--primary" href="/app">Open your workspace</a>
    <p class="story__close-note">Free for up to 10 workspaces · No card</p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* NO border-block-start and NO per-section background. See frontmatter note 1: the gap
     between sections is the separator now. */
  .story p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .story__problem-grid,
  .story__prompt-grid,
  .story__questions-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(var(--s-8), 6vw, var(--s-16));
  }

  .story__problem-grid {
    align-items: start;
  }

  .story__problem-grid > div:first-child,
  .story__problem-copy,
  .story__prompt-copy {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-content: start;
  }

  .story__problem-copy > p:last-child,
  .story__prompt-copy > p:not(.lede, .story__boundary) {
    max-inline-size: 58ch;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--t-base);
    line-height: var(--lh-base);
  }

  /* --- proof band -----------------------------------------------------------
     Three facts, hairline-separated, on the page plane. It is a <dl> because
     each item genuinely is a term and its definition, and that is what a screen
     reader should hear.

     The dividers are vertical rules between columns rather than boxes around
     them: a box would make three cards, and three cards read as three features.
     These are one statement in three parts. On a narrow screen the rules become
     horizontal for the same reason. */
     THE BAND CARRIES NO TOP PADDING OF ITS OWN. It is the evidence for the
     sentence above it, so it sits inside the pause the problem section already
     ends on rather than opening a second one; a full --pad-section on both sides
     would put 288px between a claim and its proof. The pause below it is full,
     because the next section is a different argument. */
  .story--proof {
    padding-block: 0 var(--pad-section);
  }

  .proof {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .proof__item {
    padding: var(--s-6) 0;
  }

  .proof dt {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--t-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-head);
    line-height: var(--lh-xl);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-xl);
  }

  .proof dd {
    max-inline-size: 34ch;
    margin: var(--s-2) 0 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-base);
  }

  /* --- steps ---------------------------------------------------------------
     Equal-height cards with their content packed to the top. The previous
     version reserved 18rem and pushed the title to the bottom with `margin:
     auto 0`, so each card opened on roughly 9rem of nothing; see frontmatter
     note 3. */
  .story__steps {
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--gap-grid);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .story__steps li {
    min-inline-size: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    padding: clamp(var(--s-5), 3vw, var(--s-8));
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--ring-top);
  }

  .story__number {
    color: var(--accent-bright);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-eyebrow);
  }

  .story__steps h3 {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--t-xl);
    line-height: var(--lh-xl);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-xl);
  }

  .story__steps p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-base);
  }

  /* --- prompt --------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .story__prompt-grid {
    align-items: start;
  }

  /* --stack-head is the gap before an ARTEFACT. Here the head is followed by two
     more paragraphs in the same column, so the full gap opened a hole in the
     middle of one argument. The grid's own --s-4 plus this is 32px, which is a
     paragraph break rather than a section break. */
  .story__prompt-copy .section-head {
    max-width: none;
    margin-bottom: var(--s-4);
  }

  .story__prompt-copy code {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .story__boundary {
    max-inline-size: 35rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--s-2);
    padding-inline-start: var(--s-4);
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* The disclosure moved from a caption ABOVE the prompt to a foot BELOW it. As a
     caption it was the first thing read in the panel and framed the artefact as
     fake before the reader had seen it; as a foot it answers the question the
     bracketed placeholders raise, in the order a reader meets them. */
  .story__prompt-foot {
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: var(--t-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-xs);
  }

  /* --- questions ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .story__questions-grid {
    align-items: start;
  }

  .story__questions {
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .story__questions details {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .story__questions summary {
    min-block-size: 4rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    padding-block: var(--s-4);
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--t-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .story__questions summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .story__questions summary::after {
    content: "+";
    color: var(--accent-bright);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-body);
  }

  .story__questions details[open] summary::after {
    content: "−";
  }

  .story__questions details p {
    max-inline-size: 60ch;
    padding: 0 var(--s-8) var(--s-5) 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
    line-height: var(--lh-base);
  }

  /* --- close ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .story__close {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-5);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .story__close .section-head {
    /* The head's own bottom gap is the space before an artefact. Here the next
       thing is a button, which belongs to the sentence above it. */
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .story__close .h-section {
    max-inline-size: 18ch;
  }

  .story__close .lede {
    max-inline-size: 42rem;
  }

  .story__close-note {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--t-sm);
  }

  /* --- responsive ----------------------------------------------------------- */

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .proof {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: clamp(var(--s-6), 4vw, var(--s-10));
    }

    .proof__item + .proof__item {
      padding-inline-start: clamp(var(--s-6), 4vw, var(--s-10));
      border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 47.99rem) {
    .proof__item + .proof__item {
      border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .story__problem-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    }

    .story__prompt-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.82fr) minmax(0, 1.18fr);
    }

    .story__questions-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(16rem, 0.7fr) minmax(0, 1.3fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 45rem) {
    .story__steps {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
